{"qid":"Q736","names":["Republic of Ecuador"],"title":"Republic of Ecuador","endonym":{"native":"República del Ecuador","lang":"es"},"capsule":"Ecuador became an independent republic in 1830 when it separated from Gran Colombia, with Quito as its capital and a territory corresponding roughly to the former Real Audiencia de Quito. The country lost significant Amazonian territory in the 1942 Rio Protocol following the Ecuador-Peru War, settling much of its disputed eastern border, though Ecuador repudiated the treaty in 1960 and intermittent conflict with Peru continued until the final demarcation agreed in the Brasília Accords of 1998. Oil discovered in the Oriente region from the late 1960s transformed the economy and drove development policy, while constitutional instability — including more than twenty constitutions since independence — has characterized its political history.","attestation":"Boundaries from Seshat Cliopatria (Bennett et al., Scientific Data 2025; CC BY 4.0), digitized from standard historical atlases at ~0.07° resolution. Ecuador's western and coastal borders are precisely defined; the eastern Amazonian frontier was disputed with Peru until 1998 and earlier representations reflect the contested boundary.","attestationLinks":[{"label":"Cliopatria dataset (Seshat, CC BY 4.0)","url":"https://github.com/Seshat-Global-History-Databank/cliopatria"},{"label":"Bennett et al. 2025, Scientific Data","url":"https://www.nature.com/articles/s41597-025-04516-9"}],"citations":[{"label":"David Schodt, 'Ecuador: An Andean Enigma' (Westview Press, 1987)","url":"https://archive.org/details/ecuadorandeaneni0000scho"},{"label":"Wikipedia: Ecuador","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ecuador"}],"britannica":"place/Ecuador","cowCode":null,"wikipedia":"Ecuador","rulers":[{"qid":"Q554933","name":"Juan José Flores","start":1830,"end":1834,"image":"Flórez, Juan José.jpg","imageUrl":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/Fl%C3%B3rez%2C%20Juan%20Jos%C3%A9.jpg?